 Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
 
+Kiosks have no network route during normal operation, so lookup must be fully self-contained on each kiosk with no service to install, monitor, or reach.
+
 ## Architecture
 
 Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index stored on the kiosk disk. Each kiosk builds its own index from every snapshot it accepts; no network service participates in lookup.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the SQLite index from the validated records, then activate it as a single atomic swap. Lookups continue to read the previously active index until the swap. An import failure at any stage — signature, schema version, record validation, or index build — leaves the previous snapshot and index active and reports a useful error.
+
+The index carries the snapshot date and schema version as metadata so every lookup result, including NotFound, can report the snapshot date.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. The index stores the normalized serial in an indexed column so a lookup is a single indexed equality query, meeting the 250 ms goal at 90,000 records. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+If the index file is missing or cannot be opened while the stored snapshot is intact, rebuild the index from the stored snapshot before serving lookups.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, built per kiosk from each validated snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Keeps lookup inside the kiosk with no network dependency, service timeout handling, credentials, or recurring cost, matching the offline tenet. The cost — per-kiosk index validation and rebuild on every snapshot arrival — stays entirely inside this component. The `findBySerial` interface and the certificates node are unchanged by the choice. |
+| Index activation | Build the new index alongside the old and swap atomically after validation | In-place index update | A failed or interrupted import must never degrade lookups; the last valid snapshot stays active, and rename-based swap makes the activation itself crash-safe. |
+
